---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { guides, site, sitePath } from '../../data/site';
import image from '../../../../screenshots/recording-tab.png';

const toc = [
  { id: 'wayland-support', label: 'Wayland and X11 support' },
  { id: 'install-and-check', label: 'Install and check access' },
  { id: 'first-macro', label: 'Record your first macro' },
  { id: 'compositor-limits', label: 'Coordinate and compositor limits' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Common questions' },
];
---

<GuideLayout guide={guides.wayland} image={image} imageAlt="CrossMacro recording tab with mouse and keyboard recording controls" toc={toc}>
  <section id="wayland-support">
    <h2>Wayland and X11 support</h2>
    <p>
      CrossMacro records and replays mouse and keyboard macros on Linux Wayland and X11.
      Use it for a short data-entry sequence or repeated application navigation, then
      refine actions and timing in the editor. Input access, pointer coordinates,
      screen capture, and window control are separate capabilities: support for one
      does not guarantee the others.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are coming from an <code>xdotool</code> workflow, note that X11 automation
      is not automatically compatible with native Wayland applications. CrossMacro uses
      XInput2/XTest on usable X11 sessions and different input paths on Wayland. This
      is a session-compatibility distinction, not a promise that every X11 workflow
      transfers unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="install-and-check">
    <h2>Install and check access</h2>
    <p>
      Choose a package from <a href={`${sitePath('/')}#download`}>the downloads section</a>.
      For Flatpak, with Flatpak and the Flathub remote available, run these commands
      from your desktop session:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`flatpak install flathub io.github.alper_han.crossmacro
flatpak run io.github.alper_han.crossmacro setup
flatpak run io.github.alper_han.crossmacro doctor --json --verbose`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      On Wayland, Flatpak needs writable <code>/dev/uinput</code> or
      <code>/dev/input/uinput</code> for playback and readable
      <code>/dev/input/event*</code> for recording and global hotkeys. Quick Setup
      authorizes temporary ACLs through host polkit tooling; graphical authorization
      needs a running authentication agent. Repeat setup after reboot or device
      re-enumeration if needed. Input-device access is sensitive: it exposes keyboard
      input to processes running as your user.
    </p>
    <p>
      Native Debian/Ubuntu and Fedora/RHEL packages are available from
      <a href={site.releases}>GitHub Releases</a>; download the matching architecture.
      Install only the package appropriate to your distribution:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Debian/Ubuntu: <code>sudo apt install ./crossmacro*.deb</code></li>
      <li>Fedora/RHEL: <code>sudo dnf install ./crossmacro*.rpm</code></li>
      <li>Arch Linux, stable AUR package: <code>yay -S crossmacro</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These native Wayland packages use the daemon. Run
      <code>crossmacro doctor --json --verbose</code> before changing permissions.
      Your desktop user needs <code>crossmacro</code> group membership;
      <strong>log out and back in after membership changes</strong>. If the installer
      could not add you, use <code>sudo gpasswd -a "$USER" crossmacro</code>.
      The packaged daemon targets systemd; only if activation was skipped, run
      <code>sudo systemctl enable --now crossmacro.service</code>.
      Follow the <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/dev/docs/linux.md`}>Linux reference</a>
      for AppImage, NixOS, and detailed setup diagnostics.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="first-macro">
    <h2>Record your first macro</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open a disposable text document or harmless test interface. Avoid passwords, payments, or destructive actions.</li>
      <li>Open CrossMacro and press <kbd>F8</kbd> to record. Perform a short sequence, then press <kbd>F8</kbd> again to stop.</li>
      <li>Restore the target application's starting state and focus it. Press <kbd>F9</kbd> to replay; <kbd>F10</kbd> pauses or resumes playback.</li>
      <li>Save the macro, adjust actions or timing in the editor, and test the revised sequence before using it on real work.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      These hotkeys are customizable. Keep the initial example small so that unexpected
      focus, timing, or pointer behavior is easy to spot. Once reliable, follow the
      <a href={sitePath(guides.scheduling.route)}>scheduling guide</a> to run it later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="compositor-limits">
    <h2>Coordinate and compositor limits</h2>
    <p>
      Niri currently records raw-relative pointer movement. Flatpak on COSMIC also
      records raw-relative movement; native COSMIC is not categorically relative-only.
      Native absolute behavior depends on its input backend, output geometry, and live
      cursor state. Raw device deltas are not guaranteed to equal desktop pixels.
    </p>
    <p>
      Permission to inject input does not reveal the global pointer position. Compositor
      integrations and live cursor samples determine coordinate capabilities. Flatpak
      Wayland screen reading uses Portal ScreenCast, so select every monitor needed
      for image or color checks. Consult the
      <a href={`${site.repository}/blob/dev/docs/linux.md#wayland-cursor-positioning`}>full Linux capability reference</a>
      for compositor details and the window-control matrix.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="questions">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Why can playback work while recording fails?</h3>
    <p>
      Writable uinput can enable playback while unreadable event devices prevent
      recording. Check both capabilities in your package's doctor report.
    </p>
    <h3>Will installing the host daemon fix Flatpak input?</h3>
    <p>
      No. Flatpak does not select the host daemon. Use its package-specific setup and
      doctor commands in the desktop session you intend to automate.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
